youtube.description:
How if / else works in Rust: the condition must be a bool, and if is an expression you can assign with let. Rust Day 11 of the Zero to Pro series.

📌 In this short:
✅ if / else if / else: Rust runs only the FIRST arm whose condition is true
✅ let temp = if cold { 5 } else { 25 }; works because if is an expression (both arms must have the same type)
✅ if number { } is an error in Rust (E0308, expected bool): Rust never turns numbers into true/false for you

💻 Code from the video:
let number = 6;
if number % 4 == 0 {
    println!("divides by 4");
} else if number % 3 == 0 {
    println!("divides by 3");
} else {
    println!("none of them");
}

📖 Learn more: https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch03-05-control-flow.html
